# Chronic pain trials on ClinicalTrials.gov, {P[start_year_min]}–{P[start_year_max]}

**The question.** Which interventional trials in five chronic pain conditions started between {P[start_min]} and {P[start_max]}, who runs them, why do they stop, are their results posted, and do their primary outcomes match the IMMPACT core domains?

**Data and method**
- ClinicalTrials.gov API v2 (version {N[api_version]}), pulled {N[pull_date]}: {N[n_queries]} searches, {N[pulled_unique]:,} unique trials.
- Keyword rules on conditions, titles, outcomes and inclusion criteria kept {N[included]:,} trials in {P[n_groups]} groups; headache, cancer pain and acute postoperative pain were excluded.
- Every number below is written by the code to `outputs/numbers.json`.

**Five key findings**
- **Musculoskeletal pain dominates.** Chronic low back and other musculoskeletal pain makes up {D[pct_msk]}% of trials ({N[by_group][MSK]:,}); complex regional pain syndrome only {N[by_group][CRPS]}. Starts rose from {T[starts_first_year]} in {P[start_year_min]} to {T[starts_last_year]} in {P[start_year_max]}, a count that includes planned starts ([figure](fig1_trials_per_year.png)).
- **Small, academic, mostly non-drug trials.** {D[pct_phase_not_applicable]}% have no drug phase (devices, procedures, exercise, psychological care); {D[pct_other_sponsor]}% are led by universities, hospitals or non-profits and {D[pct_industry]}% by industry. Median enrolment is {T[enrol_median]} (IQR {T[enrol_q1]}–{T[enrol_q3]}).
- **Low accrual is the commonest reason to stop.** Of {N[stop_reasons][n_stopped]} terminated, withdrawn or suspended trials, {T[stop_low_accrual]} ({T[stop_low_accrual_pct]}%) stopped mainly for low accrual, {T[stop_funding]} ({T[stop_funding_pct]}%) for funding and {T[stop_covid]} ({T[stop_covid_pct]}%) for COVID-19 ([figure](fig2_stop_reasons.png)).
- **Results are rarely posted.** Of {R[eligible]:,} completed or terminated trials whose primary completion was at least {P[results_lag_months]} months before the pull, {R[pct]}% have results posted: {T[results_pct_industry]}% for industry and {T[results_pct_other]}% for universities, hospitals and non-profits. This is not a compliance rate: the legal duty to post covers only some trials.
- **Primary outcomes centre on pain intensity.** {T[immpact_pain_pct]}% of trials have a pain-intensity primary outcome and {T[immpact_physical_pct]}% physical functioning, but only {T[immpact_emotional_pct]}% emotional functioning and {T[immpact_global_pct]}% participant ratings of improvement or satisfaction. {I[measures_none_pct]}% of primary outcome measures fit no core domain (e.g. pressure pain thresholds, opioid use).

**HK/Asia.** {A[n]} trials ({A[pct_of_included]}%) have a site in Hong Kong, mainland China, Macau, Taiwan, Japan, South Korea, Singapore, Malaysia or Thailand, led by mainland China ({T[asia_china]}), South Korea ({T[asia_korea]}) and Taiwan ({T[asia_taiwan]}). Hong Kong has {H[n]}: {T[hk_msk]} musculoskeletal and {T[hk_cpsp]} postsurgical; {T[hk_academic]} are university- or hospital-led (PolyU {T[hk_polyu]}, HKU {T[hk_hku]}, CUHK {T[hk_cuhk]}). Asian trials are larger (median enrolment {T[asia_enrol_median]} against {T[enrol_median]}) and more often industry-led ({A[pct_industry]}% against {D[pct_industry]}%).

**Limitations**
- Registry data only; publications were not checked.
- Many mainland Chinese trials register only on ChiCTR, so China is under-counted.
- Condition coding is inconsistent, so keyword rules misclassify some trials (borderline cases are listed).
- Fields are self-reported and often stale: {D[pct_unknown_status]}% have "unknown" status.
- Data are as of {N[pull_date]}.

**Next steps.** Add ChiCTR and the WHO registry platform; hand-check a random sample to measure rule accuracy; link completed trials to publications.
